# Six apps read this one file: cards and skins, then retro games, comics,
# watches and whisky. Under Docker Compose every service reads all of it, so a
# name more than one app reads is one value for all of them (the proxy and
# address ones below say "Read by every app"); the others are one app's own:
# cards' have no prefix, skins' start SKINS_, and each of the last four has its
# own. The ports are 3000 (cards), 3001 (skins), 3002 (retro games), 3003
# (comics), 3004 (watches) and 3005 (whisky). To reach the apps from a phone,
# see docs/SELF_HOSTING.md, "Phone access".

# ===========================================================================
# The card app (apps/cards)
# ===========================================================================

# --- Card identification (required for photo identification) ---
# Anthropic API key. Without it you can still add cards by hand. An OAuth
# token from `ant auth login` works in its place as ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=
# Optional: vision model used for identification (default: claude-opus-5)
# CLAUDE_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# --- Price sources (all optional) ---
# PriceCharting paid API token. In practice the only source of graded
# (PSA/BGS/CGC/SGC) prices (eBay, below, reports them too once it can search);
# covers Pokémon, Yu-Gi-Oh!, Magic and sports cards. https://www.pricecharting.com/api-documentation
PRICECHARTING_TOKEN=
# Pokémon TCG API key (free) for higher rate limits. https://dev.pokemontcg.io
POKEMONTCG_API_KEY=
# eBay sold listings (Marketplace Insights): the Production keyset of an eBay
# developer account that eBay has approved for this Limited Release API. Inert
# without both. Not verified against a live account: eBay requires a category id
# on every search, which the app does not send yet, so an approved account's
# searches are refused until it does (Settings' Test connection says so).
# https://developer.ebay.com/api-docs/buy/marketplace-insights/overview.html
# EBAY_CLIENT_ID=
# EBAY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Grading reports (optional) ---
# PSA Public API token, from your PSA account at psacard.com/publicapi. Fills a
# graded card's grade, label, population and PSA's own scans from its cert
# number (about a hundred lookups a day). PSA approves accounts for its API, so
# a new token may be refused until then; generating it accepts PSA's API
# agreement. The other grading companies are links the app opens for you.
PSA_API_TOKEN=

# --- Price history ---
# Re-price cards whose latest snapshot is older than this many hours (checked
# hourly). 0 disables. Read once at start, so a change needs a restart.
# AUTO_REFRESH_HOURS=24

# Alerts are configured in the app under Settings, including an optional
# webhook URL that each alert is POSTed to as JSON.

# --- Access (optional) ---
# Set a password to require a login before anything is reachable.
# scripts/install.sh (install.ps1 on Windows) generates one for each of the six
# apps when it creates this file.
# APP_PASSWORD=
# Optional separate signing secret. Without one, a random key is made on first
# login and kept in DATA_DIR/session-secret; set this to use a key of your own,
# a random one (openssl rand -hex 32). With sign-in with Google and no password,
# it is the only secret a session is signed with, and one shorter than 32 bytes
# signs nobody in.
# APP_SECRET=
# Sign in with Google, beside the password or in its place. Setting any
# GOOGLE_ variable turns the gate on; until all of these hold, nobody can sign
# in with Google and the log says what is missing. A Google OAuth client of
# type "Web application" whose authorised redirect URI is APP_URL followed by
# /api/auth/google/callback. APP_URL is the site's root, https (or http on
# localhost / 127.0.0.1), and sign-in with Google works at that address alone.
# It is normally the same address as APP_BASE_URL below, which says which
# requests came over https; APP_URL is where Google sends the browser back.
# APP_URL=https://cards.example.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=….apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Who may sign in, comma-separated: Google's account ids (the log names the id
# of an account it turned away), and/or addresses Google says are verified.
# Prefer ids: an id is one account for good, while for an address outside
# gmail.com and outside a Google Workspace domain, Google checked it only when
# the account was made, and a lapsed domain or a recycled mailbox can put
# another account behind it. The log names the id of an account let in by its
# address, to move here. Changing either list ends every session, as changing
# the password does.
# GOOGLE_ALLOWED_SUBS=
# GOOGLE_ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@gmail.com
# Set this when a reverse proxy in front of the app adds X-Forwarded-For and
# X-Forwarded-Proto, so the login and route limits can tell one caller from
# another and the session cookie and HSTS know the connection was secure. Both
# headers are read from the RIGHT: nginx and Caddy append rather than rewrite,
# so the rightmost entry is what the nearest proxy saw and everything left of
# it is whatever the client typed. Without this the headers are ignored
# entirely; anyone could send them. Read by every app.
# TRUST_PROXY=1
# How many proxies of your own are in front of the app, if more than one; the
# client is that many entries from the right of the chain. Setting this alone
# turns proxy trust on, as TRUST_PROXY does. Read by every app.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1
# Whether the session cookie is marked Secure. By default it follows a
# trusted proxy's X-Forwarded-Proto (never the header from anyone else); set 1
# when the app is only ever reached over https through a proxy it was not told
# about, which also refuses browser writes from a plain-http address (localhost
# and the LAN address included), or 0 to force it off. Read by every app, under
# this one name.
# COOKIE_SECURE=1
# The address the app is reached at. When it starts with https:// every
# response to that address (its name and port, as the proxy passes them in
# Host) carries Strict-Transport-Security, and its content security policy
# upgrade-insecure-requests, even where the request itself does not say it
# came over TLS, and a browser write from it must come from https. Any other
# name in ALLOWED_HOSTS, such as the LAN address you still use at home, stays
# plain http. A browser that has seen the app once will not try plain
# http again for a year on that name — and the header says
# includeSubDomains, so that applies to every name under this one as well: a
# plain-http nas.home.example.net beside an https home.example.net would be
# upgraded, and fail, in every browser that has visited the app. Set it on a
# host whose subdomains are all https, or on a leaf name of its own. Leave it
# unset for http on a LAN. Read by every app under this one name, so one value
# describes one address: apps served at different https ports (Tailscale Serve,
# say) need their own, which under Docker Compose is `environment:` per service
# in compose.override.yaml (docs/SELF_HOSTING.md, "Phone access").
# APP_BASE_URL=https://cards.example.com
# Which host names the app answers to. The default accepts localhost, any IP
# address, single-label machine names and .local/.lan/.internal/.home.arpa
# names — none of which a public DNS record can impersonate. Set this
# (comma-separated) if you reach the app by a domain name, for example through
# a reverse proxy, and have the proxy pass the original Host header through,
# port and all (nginx: proxy_set_header Host $http_host — $host drops a
# non-default port, and then every write fails the same-site check; Caddy
# passes it by default). The list replaces the default: name any LAN address
# or name you still reach the app by as well. The health check is answered
# whatever the list says. `*` disables the check. Behind a proxy that ends TLS,
# also set TRUST_PROXY, APP_BASE_URL or COOKIE_SECURE above: a browser write
# whose Origin is https is refused while the app believes it is plain http.
# Read by every app; it matches names and not ports, so one list serves all six.
# ALLOWED_HOSTS=cards.example.com

# --- Memory ---
# Request bodies are buffered whole (a restore, a photo upload), up to 64 MB
# each, and the limit is per request rather than across them: give the app
# roughly (uploads in flight x 2 x 64 MB) of headroom above its idle ~200 MB.
# compose.yaml sets a 2 GB limit per app, which covers a handful at once.

# --- Storage ---
# Directory for the SQLite database, uploaded photos and the Markdown copy
# of the collection (default: ./data, resolved from apps/cards). Ignored under
# Docker Compose, where the data directory is always the /data volume.
# DATA_DIR=./data
# The database file itself, when it should live somewhere other than DATA_DIR.
# Leave it unset under Docker Compose: unlike DATA_DIR it is not pinned there, and
# would move the database out of the /data volume.
# DATABASE_FILE=./data/collectcollect.db
# Every card is also written as a Markdown file under DATA_DIR/collection so the
# collection outlives the app. Set to "off" for a read-only data volume. The four
# apps at the end of this file read it too, under this name; skins has its own.
# MARKDOWN_MIRROR=on

# ===========================================================================
# The CS2 skins app (apps/skins)
# ===========================================================================
# Its own variables throughout, so both apps can run side by side from one
# shell without either one pointing at the other's collection.

# --- Access (optional) ---
# Its own password and its own cookie. Cookies are scoped to a host and not to
# a port, so sharing one variable would let a session for one app unlock the
# other. scripts/install.sh generates one when it creates this file.
# SKINS_APP_PASSWORD=
# Optional separate signing secret. Without one, a random key is made on first
# login and kept in SKINS_DATA_DIR/session-secret; set this to use your own,
# random and, with sign-in with Google and no password, at least 32 bytes.
# SKINS_APP_SECRET=
# Sign in with Google, as in the card app with its own variables (the redirect
# URI is SKINS_APP_URL followed by /api/auth/google/callback; one Google client
# may carry both apps' addresses). APP_BASE_URL, above, is read by both apps
# under that one name; this one is the skins app's own.
# SKINS_APP_URL=https://skins.example.com
# SKINS_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=….apps.googleusercontent.com
# SKINS_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# SKINS_GOOGLE_ALLOWED_SUBS=
# SKINS_GOOGLE_ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@gmail.com

# --- Storage ---
# Directory for the SQLite database and the Markdown copy of the inventory
# (default: ./data, resolved from apps/skins). Ignored under Docker Compose,
# where the data directory is always the /data volume.
# SKINS_DATA_DIR=./data
# The database file itself, when it should live somewhere other than SKINS_DATA_DIR.
# Leave it unset under Docker Compose, as DATABASE_FILE above.
# SKINS_DATABASE_FILE=./data/collectcollect-skins.db
# Every item is also written as a Markdown file under SKINS_DATA_DIR/collection.
# Its own switch: the card app's MARKDOWN_MIRROR has no effect here.
# SKINS_MARKDOWN_MIRROR=on

# --- Price sources ---
# Steam and Skinport need no key and are always on. Skinport returns its whole
# catalogue in one request; Steam answers about twenty times a minute, so a
# large inventory takes a while to price.
#
# CSFloat needs a key. It is also the only source that knows an item's float
# without inspecting it in game. https://csfloat.com/profile/developer
# CSFLOAT_API_KEY=

# A price you type in on an item overrides every source, on that item.

# --- Price history ---
# Re-price anything whose latest price is older than this many hours (checked
# hourly). 0 disables. A large inventory takes a while, which is fine here:
# it runs in the background and skips anything already fresh. Read once at
# start, so a change needs a restart.
# SKINS_AUTO_REFRESH_HOURS=24

# ===========================================================================
# Building
# ===========================================================================
# Set when building the Docker image: produces the self-contained server the
# image runs. `next start` does not use it, so a normal build leaves it unset.
# BUILD_STANDALONE=1

# ===========================================================================
# The domain-engine apps: retro games, comics, watches, whisky
# ===========================================================================
# Each has its own prefix so all of them can run side by side from one shell.
# They share ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN / CLAUDE_MODEL (photo
# identification), MARKDOWN_MIRROR and, for retro games and comics,
# PRICECHARTING_TOKEN, with the card app above. The variables of each, with
# <PREFIX> its prefix:
#
#   <PREFIX>_APP_PASSWORD        Its own password and its own cookie. The gate is
#                                the password alone: no sign-in with Google, and
#                                signing out clears one browser's cookie, so
#                                change the password to end every session.
#                                scripts/install.sh writes one for every app when
#                                it creates this file, and will not publish an app
#                                without one to your network (--lan).
#   <PREFIX>_APP_SECRET          Optional. Without one a random key is made on
#                                first login and kept in the app's data directory
#                                as session-secret, and sessions are signed with
#                                that; set one to use a key of your own, a random
#                                one (openssl rand -hex 32). The key is not part
#                                of a backup.
#   <PREFIX>_DATA_DIR            Where the SQLite database, uploaded photos and
#                                the Markdown copy (collection/) live
#                                (default: ./data, resolved from the app). For a
#                                shell run: under Docker Compose it is ignored,
#                                and the data directory is always the /data volume.
#   <PREFIX>_DATABASE_FILE       The database file itself, when it should live
#                                somewhere other than <PREFIX>_DATA_DIR
#                                (default: <PREFIX>_DATA_DIR/collectcollect-<app>.db).
#                                Leave it unset under Docker Compose, which does
#                                not pin it: it would move the database out of
#                                the /data volume.
#   <PREFIX>_AUTO_REFRESH_HOURS  Re-price anything whose latest price is older
#                                than this many hours (checked hourly, the first
#                                time a minute after the app starts). 0 disables.
#                                Read once at start, so a change needs a restart.

# --- Retro games (apps/retro-games, port 3002) ---
# RETRO_GAMES_APP_PASSWORD=
# RETRO_GAMES_APP_SECRET=
# RETRO_GAMES_DATA_DIR=./data
# RETRO_GAMES_DATABASE_FILE=./data/collectcollect-retro-games.db
# RETRO_GAMES_AUTO_REFRESH_HOURS=24

# --- Comics (apps/comics, port 3003) ---
# COMICS_APP_PASSWORD=
# COMICS_APP_SECRET=
# COMICS_DATA_DIR=./data
# COMICS_DATABASE_FILE=./data/collectcollect-comics.db
# COMICS_AUTO_REFRESH_HOURS=24

# --- Watches (apps/watches, port 3004) ---
# No market price source is wired up; values are entered by hand.
# WATCHES_APP_PASSWORD=
# WATCHES_APP_SECRET=
# WATCHES_DATA_DIR=./data
# WATCHES_DATABASE_FILE=./data/collectcollect-watches.db
# WATCHES_AUTO_REFRESH_HOURS=24

# --- Whisky (apps/whisky, port 3005) ---
# No auction price source is wired up; values are entered by hand.
# WHISKY_APP_PASSWORD=
# WHISKY_APP_SECRET=
# WHISKY_DATA_DIR=./data
# WHISKY_DATABASE_FILE=./data/collectcollect-whisky.db
# WHISKY_AUTO_REFRESH_HOURS=24

# MARKDOWN_MIRROR=off (above) switches off the plain-text copy of all four, for a
# read-only data volume.
